ZFS import/export ARC regression

export.py exercises the parallel ARC eviction and asynchronous pool flush
paths by importing a disposable pool, checking every regular file of an
extracted source archive, and exporting the pool repeatedly without an idle
period.  Reading the tree populates the ARC; successive exports can overlap
the previous pool instance's asynchronous flush and background eviction.

This caught a mismatch between OpenZFS taskq_wait() and native taskq barriers:
concurrent barriers could return with entries still queued, allowing ARC to
redispatch or free them.  The observed panic was:

    VERIFY(zfs_taskq_empty_ent(ent))
    zfs_taskq_dispatch_ent -> arc_evict_state -> arc_flush_impl -> arc_flush_task

Requires Python 3.11 or later and root.  This standalone test is not included
in the recursive uvm regress run.  It imports and exports the entire named
pool, which must be disposable and initially exported.  It does not create
or destroy pools, datasets, or files.

Prepare a dataset with its mountpoint configured, extract an uncompressed
tar archive beneath MOUNTPOINT/tree, then export the pool.  Use a large source
tree (the measured fixture has 86,196 files and 1.44 GiB of file contents), at
least six vCPUs to exercise parallel ARC eviction, and multiple iterations:

    python3 /usr/src/regress/sys/uvm/zfs_export/export.py \
        fsbench/bench /dev/sd1c /mnt/bench /root/src.tar --iterations 12

Run under a timeout on the VM's host and capture the serial console, since a
kernel panic can block the guest process.  Each completed iteration reports
its verified file count, elapsed time, and number of transient export EBUSY
retries.  All retries are bounded; export is never forced.  Any mismatch or
command failure aborts the test and leaves the filesystem for diagnosis.
Success leaves the pool exported.
